<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">University of Turin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1404">1404</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> is one of the oldest universities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, founded in 1404 under Prince Louis of Savoy-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> with papal approval. It moved several times in its early centuries and was reorganised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a> in the 1720s as a state university. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erasmus">Erasmus</a> took his doctorate in theology there in 1506.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/university-of-turin.jpg" alt="University of Turin" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: University of Turin · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:University_of_Turin_Sigil.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>At the start of the fifteenth century, instability in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> and the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Gian Galeazzo Visconti</a> led the teaching staff of the universities of Pavia and Piacenza to propose a new studium generale to Ludovico di Savoia-Acaia. Turin was chosen because it lay at the crossroads of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liguria">Liguria</a> and Lombardy, was an episcopal seat, and because the Savoy prince was willing to found a university on his own land. In autumn 1404 a bull of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avignon">Avignon</a> pope Benedict XIII marked its birth. Emperor Sigmund confirmed it in 1412, and the antipope John XXIII in 1413.</p><p>At first it taught only civil and canon law and could confer the licentia and doctoratus. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>, as rector of studies, proclaimed and conferred the title on new doctors.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The early decades were discontinuous, marked by epidemics and crises and by difficult relations with the local administration. The university moved to Chieri and then to Savigliano in the 1420s and 1430s. Returning to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a> in 1436, it was reordered under ducal licences that set up faculties of Theology, Arts and Medicine, and Law, with twenty-five chairs. The invasion of Savoy by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> closed it in 1536, and it was re-established in Turin in 1566 by Duke Emmanuel Philibert.</p><p>Plague, famine and war caused decline in the second half of the seventeenth century. The new premises in via Po opened for the academic year 1720 to 1721, the first of the reform of studies by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/victor-amadeus-ii">Victor Amadeus II</a>, who held that a university directly controlled by the state would train a faithful and capable ruling class. The reform created the Collegio delle Province, housing one hundred young people of low social origin at the state's expense, and a chair of Italian rhetoric beside that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1864 Filippo de Filippi gave the first lecture in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> on the theories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-darwin">Charles Darwin</a>. Giulio Bizzozero founded the Laboratory of General Pathology in 1873 and discovered blood platelets, and in 1884 Carlo Forlanini tried out the first artificial lung in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turin">Turin</a>. Under French imperial rule it became the second largest university in the empire after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. The first Italian chair of psychology was held there by Friedrich Kiesow in 1905.</p><p>In the twentieth century it was a centre of Italian anti-fascism. In the 1930s Giuseppe Levi trained Salvador Luria, Renato Dulbecco and Rita Levi-Montalcini, each of whom won a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nobel-prize">Nobel Prize</a> in Medicine or Physiology. Antonio Gramsci, Piero Gobetti, Palmiro Togliatti, Primo Levi and Italo Calvino are among its graduates, and Giuseppe Peano and Umberto Eco taught there.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “University of Turin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He carried out a general land survey to tax noble and church land, reformed the <b>University of Turin</b>, reduced feudal privileges and issued the Royal Constitutions of 1723 and 1729, a consolidated code.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-savoy">Duchy of Savoy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>&quot;Giovinezza&quot; (&quot;Youth&quot;) was composed by the lawyer and composer Giuseppe Blanc in 1909 as &quot;Il Commiato&quot; (&quot;Farewell&quot;), a graduation song of the <b>University of Turin</b>.</blockquote><a href="/songs/giovinezza">Giovinezza</a></li><li><blockquote>Prince Louis founded the <b>University of Turin</b> in 1404.</blockquote><a href="/state/lordship-of-piedmont">Lordship of Piedmont</a></li></ul></section>
